## 4. Theoretical findings already incorporated

### 4.1 Abandon the net-exergy/institutional-complexity master ratio

Valid. The ratio lacks a stable dimension, hides bottlenecks, and can generate perverse interpretations. The master architecture instead uses typed constraints, viability kernels, and margins.

### 4.2 Noncompensatory vector margins

Valid. Componentwise constraints or minimum margins are preferable to an unrestricted weighted sum when critical components are noncompensable.

### 4.3 Typed physical, normative, and conjectural statements

Valid. Conservation laws, normative commitments, definitions, empirical regularities, and conjectures must remain distinct.

### 4.4 Institutional adequacy requires more than “variety”

Valid. Observation, identifiability, communication, decision, actuation, latency, compliance, legitimacy, and learning are distinct modules.

### 4.5 Multi-horizon analysis

Valid. A universal 75–100-year horizon is not defensible. Horizons depend on response, regeneration, lock-in, irreversibility, and continuation conditions.

### 4.6 Regenerative/generative capacity

Valid as an architectural category. Persistence requires maintenance and regeneration of critical stocks, capacities, knowledge, institutions, and ecological processes. This is represented in the master through capacities, regeneration, maintenance, and transition viability.

### 4.7 Distribution and legitimacy

Valid as normative and relational constraints. They are not derivable from thermodynamics.

### 4.8 Policy-family comparison through viability

Potentially valid. Policy families can be compared through their implementable policy classes and resulting viable regions, but only after common dynamics, constraints, information, disturbances, and normative standards are specified.

