## 6. Scaling claims requiring correction

### 6.1 Kleiber versus Bettencourt

It is correct that organismal Kleiber scaling should not be transferred automatically to cities or economies. It is not correct to replace it with one universal socioeconomic exponent \(\beta\approx1.15\).

Bettencourt-type exponents are empirical, indicator-, sample-, boundary-, and period-dependent. They should enter as model-family hypotheses with uncertainty, alternative models, and held-out testing.

### 6.2 Superlinear singularity claims

The claim that superlinear scaling necessarily produces a finite-time singularity depends on the chosen growth equation and absence of saturation, adaptation, resource limits, or structural change. It is not a general consequence of an estimated cross-sectional scaling exponent.

### 6.3 S-curve family

Valid correction: logistic saturation is one possible trajectory among overshoot, collapse, oscillation, switching, substitution, and displacement. The trajectory must be derived from specified dynamics.

