## 9. Evaluation of the five proposed falsifiable claims

### Claim 1 — Thermodynamic accounting

Useful after replacing “thermodynamic” with typed material/energy accounting and defining boundary completeness, uncertainty, and the statistical test. A residual beyond \(2\sigma\) may indicate omitted flows, measurement error, or model discrepancy; it does not uniquely falsify the ledger architecture.

### Claim 2 — Non-aggregation

The proposed falsifier is incorrect. Finding one system where a scalar says “sustainable” and all component constraints are satisfied does not falsify the claim that scalar aggregates can conceal violations.

A valid test would compare scalar classification with componentwise constraints across a preregistered sample and estimate false-safe classifications. A universal scalar certificate requires proof of restricted-domain equivalence.

### Claim 3 — Institutional delay

The claim is too broad. Delay effects depend on sign, gain, plant dynamics, observation, sampling, and policy. The corrected hypothesis should be model-class-specific and use independently estimated lag components.

### Claim 4 — Viability-kernel membership

Potentially valid but requires:

- estimated kernels with uncertainty;
- comparable policy and disturbance exposure;
- current-state classification error;
- specified survival horizon;
- avoidance of using the same outcomes to estimate and test the kernel.

Outside-kernel states can remain safe under favorable disturbances or finite horizons, so the comparison must match the kernel semantics.

### Claim 5 — Mixed policy families

As written, the hypothesis may be tautological if the “mixed” policy class contains the pure classes. Policy-set monotonicity then guarantees a weakly larger kernel. A nontrivial test must impose equal information, authority, complexity, cost, and action budgets or compare non-nested policy families.

