## 10. “Present world-hook” and toy-model requirements

World contact is essential for empirical claims, but not every mathematical definition or theorem needs a current dataset. A mathematical theorem needs explicit assumptions, proof, counterexamples, and scope. An architectural conjecture needs a disproof route and prospective application class. An empirical claim needs observations and identification.

Toy models are valuable for exposing hidden assumptions, but they do not by themselves validate generality. They should be surprising enough to produce counterexamples, not designed only to reproduce the verbal claim.

